.TH "TDDA SERIAL" 1 "May 2026" "3.0" "tdda serial manual"
.SH NAME
\fBtdda serial\fR — Converts and generates serial metadata files.

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtdda serial\fR [FLAGS] \fBinmetadata\fR \fBoutmetadata\fR
.br
\fBtdda serial\fR \fB--to FMT\fR [FLAGS] \fBinmetadata\fR \fBoutmetadata\fR

.PP
Converts metadata from one metadata format, in \fBinpath\fR,
to another, in \fBoutpath\fR\&.

.PP
\fBtdda serial\fR [FLAGS] \fBindata\fR \fBoutmetadata\fR

.PP
Creates metadata for \fBindata\fR in \fBoutmetadata\fR

.PP
\fBtdda serial\fR [FLAGS] \fBinmetadata\fR \fBscript.py\fR

.PP
Creates Python code for reading a file in the format in \fBinmetadata\fR as
Python. Often, a reading library would be specified, e.g.

.PP
\fBtdda serial\fR \fBa.serial\fR \fBa.py\fR \fB--to pd.r\fR

.PP
which specifies that the Python script should use \fBpandas.read_csv\fR\&.

.PP
Supported formats \fBFMT\fR:

.PP
SHORT FORM  LONG FORM/Description
  \fB\&.\fR         \fBtdda.serial\fR
  \fBpd.r\fR      \fBpandas.read_csv\fR
  \fBpd.w\fR      \fBpandas.DataFrame.to_csv\fR
  \fBpl.r\fR      \fBpolars.read_csv\fR
  \fBpl.w\fR      \fBpolars.DataFrame.write_csv\fR
  \fBcsv.r\fR     \fBpython.csv.reader\fR
  \fBcsv.w\fR     \fBpython.csv.writer\fR
  \fBcsvw\fR      \fBCSVW\fR
  \fBfl\fR        \fBfrictionless\fR
  \fBfless\fR     \fBfrictionless\fR
  \fBfl.r\fR      \fBfrictionless.resource\fR
  \fBfl.p\fR      \fBfrictionless.package\fR

.PP
Multiple formats can be separated by commas.

.PP
Format is usually inferred from filename if following common conventions
for tdda.serial, CSVW, and frictionless.

.SH OPTIONS
\fB--to FMT\fR               Specify output metadata format (see list of
                       formats above)

.PP
\fB-B BE, --backend BE\fR    Specify backend for Pandas flavours:
                           \fBn\fR: \fBnumpy_nullable\fR
                           \fBa\fR: \fBpyarrow\fR
                           \fBo\fR: \fBoriginal\fR Pandas backend.

.PP
\fB--for FILE\fR             Filename for data to use when generating CSVW
                       or Frictionless data.
                       (Can also be used for \fBtdda.serial\fR and \fB\&.py\fR
                       output)

.PP
\fB-N, --no-config\fR        Use default configuration (ignore \fB~/.tdda.toml\fR)

.PP
\fB-g, --gen, --generate\fR  Generate (infer) metadata for flat file

.PP
\fB-q, --quiet\fR            Quiet output

.PP
\fB-v, --verbose\fR          Verbose output

.PP
\fB-V, --Verbose\fR          More verbose output

.SH Options used primarily or exclusively with \fB--generate\fR/\fB--gen\fR/\fB-g\fR
\fB--sep D, --delimiter D\fR     Specify \fBD\fR as the field separator.

.PP
\fB--quote-char Q, --quote Q\fR  Specify \fBQ\fR as the quote character.
                           (Q is always \fB"\fR or \fB\&'\fR in practice.)

.PP
\fB--nulls S\fR                  Specify null indicator, or comma-separated
                           list of null indicators.

.PP
\fB--escape\fR                   Use backslash as escape character.
                           \fBNOTE:\fP Always backslash: does not take
                           argument.

.PP
\fB--no-escape\fR                Do not support backslash escaping with \fB-g\fR\&.
                           \fBNOTE:\fP This only affects quotes, separators,
                           and backslashes. Standard escapes for
                           control sequences (\\t, \\n, \\r, \\f)
                           are always supported.

.PP
\fB--stutter\fR                  Specify quote stuttering.
                           Usually an alternative to \fB--escape\fR\&.

.PP
\fB--no-stutter\fR               Do not use quote stuttering.
                           Usually used with \fB--escape\fR\&.

.PP
\fB--encoding ENC, -e ENC\fR     Specify \fBENC\fR as encoding.

.PP
\fB--date-format D\fR            Specify \fBD\fR as the (file-wide default)
                           date format.

.PP
\fB--datetime-format D\fR        Specify \fBD\fR as the (file-wide default) format
                           for \fBdatetime\fR fields.

.PP
\fB--sample-lines N, -n N\fR     Use (up to) \fBN\fR sample lines when inferring
                           metadata.

.PP
\fB--single-field, -1\fR         Inform the metadata inferred that the file
                           contains only a single field (column).

.PP
\fB--include-path\fR             Include \fBpath\fR in \fB\&.serial\fR output

.PP
\fB--exclude-path\fR             Do not include in \fB\&.serial\fR output

.PP
\fB--quoting Q\fR                Set \fBquoting\fR to \fBQ\fR\&. \fBQ\fR must be one of:
                             \fBQUOTE_ALL\fR
                             \fBQUOTE_MINIMAL\fR
                             \fBQUOTE_NONNUMERIC\fR
                             \fBQUOTE_NONE\fR
                             \fBQUOTE_NOTNULL\fR
                             \fBQUOTE_STRINGS\fR
                             \fBQUOTE_STRINGS_ONLY\fR

.PP
\fB--use-literal-dates\fR        Specifies that date formats should be
                           written to \fB\&.serial\fR files with unambiguous
                           literal examples such as \fB2000-12-31T12:34:56\fR\&.

.PP
\fB--use-yyyy-dates\fR           Specifies that date formats should be
                           written to \fB\&.serial\fR files in the form
                           exemplified by \fBYYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS\fR\&.

.PP
\fB--use-pc-dates\fR             Specifies that date formats should be
                           written to \fB\&.serial\fR files in Python
                           \fBstrftime\fR-compatible % formats, exemplified
                           by \fB%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S\fR\&.

.SH EXAMPLES
1) \fBtdda serial a.csv a.serial\fR
.br
    Generate tdda.serial metadata describing format of \fBa.csv\fR
    in \fBa.serial\fR

.PP
2) \fBtdda serial --to . a.csv a.serial\fR
.br
    Same as previous, explicitly specifying the default, \fBtdda.serial\fR,
    output format (\fB\&.\fR is short for \fBtdda.serial\fR format).

.PP
3) \fBtdda serial a.csv a-metadata.json\fR
.br
    Generate CSVW metadata describing format of \fBa.csv\fR
    in \fBa-metadata.json\fR

.PP
4) \fBtdda serial --to csvw a.csv a.json\fR
.br
    Same as previous, explicitly specifying format with non-standard
    output name

.PP
5) \fBtdda serial a.serial a-metadata.json\fR
.br
    Converts \fBtdda.serial\fR metadata to CSVW

.PP
6) \fBtdda serial a-metadata.json a.serial\fR
.br
    Converts CSVW metadata to \fBtdda.serial\fR

.SH USING SERIAL METADATA WITH TDDA COMMANDS
For all tdda command-line commands, and in most places within
API calls where CSV or other flat file is specified, there is the
option to specify the file format using \fBtdda.serial\fR files,
CSVW files, or Frictionless files. This is based on the \fB:\fR (colon) specifier.

.PP
When specifying a path to a CSV (or other flat) file:
.IP \(bu 2
If the path is used by itself, the \fBtdda\fR library will use
either \fBtdda.serial.csv_to_pandas\fR or \fBtdda.serial.csv_to_polars\fR
to read it into a DataFrame. The default is currently pandas
(with the \fBnumpy_nullable\fR back end), but this can be
configured (see \fBtdda config\fR)
or, in many cases controlled with command line flags
(\fB--polars\fR, \fB--pandas\fR, \fB--backend BACKEND\fR (for Pandas only)).
.IP \(bu 2
If the path ends in a colon (e.g. \fBfoo.csv:\fR), TDDA will search
for metadata in the same directory as the file and, if it finds
one, pass that to the appropriate \fBcsv_to_...\fR function for
more accurate DataFrame generation.
.IP \(bu 2
In doing this, it will look for the following in priority order,
given a file \fBfoo.csv\fR:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfoo.csv.serial\fR (\fBtdda.serial\fR metadata)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfoo.serial\fR (\fBtdda.serial\fR metadata). This is actually more
common than the previous form, but if there are multiple files
with different extensions, the former is more specific, so is
checked first.
.IP \(bu 2
Anything that matches foo using \fB@\fR as a wildcard, e.g.
\fB@.serial\fR, \fBf@.serial\fR, \fBf@o.serial\fR, \fB@oo.serial\fR\&.
(\fB@\fR acts like \fB*\fR in the shell, while avoiding needing
\fB*\fR in filenames, which can be awkward.)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfoo-metadata.json\fR, \fBfoo-csvmetadata.json\fR, \fBfoo-csv-metadata.json\fR,
\fBfoo.csvmetadata.json\fR, \fBfoo.csv-metadata.json\fR
(all of which are common conventions for CSVW metadata files).
.IP \(bu 2
The same CSVW patterns with \fB@\fR wildcards
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfoo.serial.json\fR, \fBfoo.serial.yaml\fR, \fBfoo.resource.json\fR,
\fBfoo.resource.yaml\fR, \fBfoo.package.json\fR, \fBfoo.package.yaml\fR,
all of which are common for Frictionless metadata files.
.IP \(bu 2
The same patterns for \fBserial\fR or \fBpackage\fR frictionless files
with \fB@\fR wildcards. Wildcards are not searched in \fBresource\fR files,
because in frictionless these always correspond to a single
data file.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
If the path contains a colon, the part to the right of the colon
will be interpreted as a metadata file. So \fBfoo.csv:bar.serial\fR
will use \fBbar.serial\fR\&.

.SH BUGS
The \fBtdda serial\fR functionality is fairly new, and there are probably
still bugs and undesirable features in the implementation.

.SH SEE ALSO
Test Driven Data Analysis
